     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"#954F72\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hi Luke,</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I think you want to look at the control.fixed option, which allows you to specify priors for the fixed effects (both a default and specific ones) and also for the intercept.</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">However, I believe that fixed effect priors may need to be Gaussian to satisfy the constraints of the INLA process.  I have not found this to be a limiting feature as the latent effects (random effects, autoregressive effects, splines, etc.) seem to be where the shape of the priors make more difference, and some fixed effects perhaps could be re-specified as versions of latent effects to benefit from that option.</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Jonathan</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Sent from <a href=\"https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId=550986\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkId%3D550986&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792440935469000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3WHH2TASz2FwoMcvsf-GZQ\">Mail</a> for Windows 10</p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"#954F72\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><div style=\"border:none;border-top:solid #e1e1e1 1.0pt;padding:3.0pt 0in 0in 0in\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"border:none;padding:0in\"><b>From: </b><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">LukeM</a><br><b>Sent: </b>Thursday, May 14, 2020 9:57 AM<br><b>To: </b><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">R-inla discussion group</a><br><b>Subject: </b>[r-inla] different priors for GLM</p></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hi to all,</p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I&#39;m new at INLA, I&#39;m studying on the official documentation and apply some techniques to a problem of logistic regression model.</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I have 4 variables in my dataframe df: OUTCOME (0/1) that is my dependent variable Y, AGE, CLASS, INCOME and I would like to fit a model like:</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">logit(y_i) = beta_0 + beta_1 * AGE + beta_2 *CLASS + beta_3 * INCOME</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">As I would do in Jags, I would like to put a Cauchy (0,10) prior on beta_0 and t-student on beta_1, beta_2 and beta_3.</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I&#39;m quite confused when facing up with random and fixed effect on the priors. How can I explicit separate priors for intercept and coefficient of the covariates?</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I did:</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7;border-width:initial;box-sizing:border-box;white-space:pre-wrap;break-inside:avoid;overflow:auto\"><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt\">HT.prior =</span><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt\"> &quot;expression:</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre></div><div><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7;box-sizing:border-box;white-space:pre-wrap;break-inside:avoid;border-width:initial;overflow:auto\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">  sigma = exp(-theta/2);</span></span><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u></span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">  nu = 3;</span></span><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u></span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">  log_dens = 0 - 0.5 * log(nu * pi) - (-0.1207822);</span></span><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u></span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">  log_dens = log_dens - 0.5 * (nu + 1) * log(1 + sigma * sigma);</span></span><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u></span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">  log_dens = log_dens - log(2) - theta / 2;</span></span><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u></span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">  return(log_dens);</span></span><code><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\"><u></u><u></u></span></code></pre><pre style=\"background:#f7f7f7\"><span><span style=\"font-family:Consolas;color:#4070a0;letter-spacing:.15pt;border:none windowtext 1.0pt;padding:0in\">&quot;</span></span><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:Consolas;color:#333333;letter-spacing:.15pt\"><u></u><u></u></span></pre></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">inla_model &lt;- inla ( Y ~ AGE + CLASS + INCOME , data=df, family=&#39;binomial&#39;,control_family=list=(hyper=<span style=\"font-size:9.5pt;font-family:Consolas;letter-spacing:.15pt\">HT.prior</span>)).</p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">But It doesn&#39;t work.. where do I have to put prior on intercept and coefficients of covariates?</p></div></div></div></div>"
