   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi,\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; the priors I would put on intercept is a Cauchy(0,10) and a t-student (0,0.03) with 1 degree of freedom for the coefficients of the covariates.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I try doing:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; inla_model &lt;- inla (y ~ f(1,model=&#39;linear&#39;,, ) + f(AGE,model=&#39;linear&#39;) + f(CLASS,model=&#39;linear&#39;)+ f(INCOME, model=&#39;linear) -1 ,  data=df, family=&#39;binomial&#39; )\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; but where do I have to explicit the priors (and their parameters in the f()  supposing to fix location=0 and scale=10 aand the same procedure for t-student)? :(\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Priors are defined in the ‘hyper’ argument inside the f() function. Before doing anything else, please, make sure that you know how priors are set in INLA [1] and try to use any of the other priors implemented in R-INLA for testing. If you use the muparser to define the prior, then the prior parameters will be set in the muparser code. \r<br>\r<br>Best,\r<br>\r<br>Virgilio\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>[1] <a href=\"https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/inla-gitbook/ch-priors.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/inla-gitbook/ch-priors.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792440935478000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2SPfojWYV2dlz8qBn-ByqP\">https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/inla-gitbook/ch-priors.html</a>"
